{{Quote|How am I to know the good side from the bad?''"<br />"''You will ''know'', when you are calm. At peace, passive.|Luke Skywalker and Yoda|<ref name="Episode V">{{Film|V}}</ref>}}
[[File:Light_side_symbol.svg|thumb|right|200px|Ancient symbol of the light side of the Force]]
The '''light side of the Force''', also known as '''Ashla''',<ref name="Steps Into Shadow">''[[Star Wars Rebels: Steps Into Shadow]]''</ref> was one of two aspects of [[the Force]]. The light side was aligned with calmness, peace, and passiveness, and was used mainly for knowledge and defense.<ref name="Episode V" /> The [[Jedi]] were notable practitioners of the light, being selfless servants of the will of the Force,<ref name="AEYNTK">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]''</ref> and their enemies, the [[Sith]] followed the [[dark side of the Force]].<ref name="Episode I">{{Film|I}}</ref>

==Essence of the light side==
===Selflessness===
{{Quote|You don't understand what you're asking me to do.''"<br />"''Yes, I do; you can't save your master, and I can't save mine. I'm asking you to let go.|Ezra Bridger is guided by [[Ahsoka Tano]] to let go|<ref name="A World Between Worlds">{{Rebels|A World Between Worlds}}</ref>}}
[[File:LoveIsLettingGo-2016DoctorAphra40.jpg|thumb|left|300px|The light side of the Force was selflessness; the Jedi's purpose was exemplified by their acts of altruism and letting go.]]
The light side of [[the Force]] was selflessness. Its [[Jedi]] practitioners [[Living Force|lived in the present]],<ref name="Episode I" /> [[Meditation|clearing their mind]], letting go of their [[Sentience|consciousness]], and listening to the Force, acting on instinct.<ref name="Episode IV">{{Film|IV}}</ref> The [[Jedi Order]], which was devoted to the light side of the Force,<ref name="SotJ">''[[Star Wars: The Secrets of the Jedi]]''</ref> had the core principle of being the guardians of peace and justice,<ref name="LotJ">''[[The High Republic: Light of the Jedi]]''</ref> an example of which was by their altruism such as during the [[High Republic Era]].<ref name="Sskeer">{{SWYouTube|TPeRcyJE5dk|Sskeer &#124; Characters of Star Wars the High Republic|sw_url=video/sskeer-characters-of-star-wars-the-high-republic}}</ref> As decreed by the [[Jedi Code]],<ref name="Episode II">{{Film|II}}</ref> Jedi were committed to compassion&mdash;unconditional love for all living things.<ref name="Episode I" /> Compassionate actions extended to [[Death|dying]], as a member of the Jedi Order believed in loss in the service of a greater good. Recognizing this gave one mastery in the Force, allowing a Jedi to repel spirit.<ref name="SW 2020 6">[[Star Wars (2020) 6|''Star Wars'' (2020) 6]]</ref> The [[anchorite sect]], bound to the Order and aligned with the light side of the Force, believed that suffering was the basis of life; that one should not ask for a life of any merit, for self-sacrifice is reward enough.<ref name="Empire's End">''[[Aftermath: Empire's End]]''</ref>

The Force did not ''belong'' to the Jedi or any other organization&mdash;some individuals who were not part of any [[religion]] also believed in selflessness. Such included the [[Spectres]] rebel cell, whose leader, [[Hera Syndulla]], taught the young [[Ezra Bridger]] that when one finds people who need one's help, one helps them no matter what, and that if all one does is fight for their own life, then one's life is worth nothing.<ref name="Spark">''[[Star Wars Rebels: Spark of Rebellion]]''</ref>

===Balance of the Force===
====Description====
{{Quote|We call upon the Three. Light. Dark. And Balance true. One is no greater than the others. Together, they unite, restore, center, and renew. We walk into the light, acknowledge the dark, and find balance within ourselves. The Force is strong.|Incantation recited by [[Dooku]], [[Lene Kostana]], and [[Sifo-Dyas]]|<ref name="Dooku: Jedi Lost">''[[Dooku: Jedi Lost]]''</ref>}}
[[File:Immortal.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Those who have wholly devoted themselves to the Force were able to retain their consciousness after death, achieving immortality by becoming one with the Cosmic Force.]]
Balance in the Force&mdash;harmony between all life&mdash;was achieved and maintained through understanding and accepting the dark side of the Force that exists within oneself, while serving the light side of the Force; practitioners of this method, namely Jedi, devoted themselves to the will of the Force,<ref name="AEYNTK" /><ref name="Episode V" /> acting in accordance to the symbiotic relationship between the [[Living Force]] and the [[Cosmic Force]]&mdash;the [[Death|transformation]] of the energy of all life to the greater energy field that bound everything&mdash;by submitting to the Cosmic Force and what it communicated to them through the [[midi-chlorian]]s.<ref name="Voices">{{TCW|Voices}}</ref> The [[dark side adept]] [[Snoke]] believed that, when the dark side grew in power, the light would naturally increase in strength as its counterbalance. Indeed, when Snoke's apprentice [[Ben Solo|Kylo Ren]] grew in power,<ref name="Episode VIII">{{Film|VIII}}</ref> a young woman named [[Rey Skywalker|Rey]] discovered her [[Force power]]s<ref name="Episode VII">{{Film|VII}}</ref> and stood against him as his equal.<ref name="Episode VIII" />

====History====
=====The Chosen One=====
{{Main|Chosen One}}
{{Quote|A Chosen One shall come, born of no father, and through him will ultimate balance in the Force be restored.|A Jedi prophecy|<ref name="Master & Apprentice">''[[Master & Apprentice]]''</ref>}}
[[File:The-Chosen-Ones-Chooses-Nature.png|thumb|left|300px|Anakin Skywalker was a Jedi Knight and the prophesied Chosen One destined to bring balance to the Force.]]
Prior to the fall of the [[Galactic Republic]], there was an ancient prophecy that foretold of a being that would bring balance to the Force: the [[Chosen One]].<ref name="Episode I" /> The [[Jedi Order]] believed the Chosen One would destroy the [[Sith]] and bring balance to the Force.<ref name="Episode III">{{Film|III}}</ref> [[Jedi Master]] [[Qui-Gon Jinn]] discovered a [[human]] boy on [[Tatooine]] with no father&mdash;[[Anakin Skywalker]]&mdash;and believed him to be possibly conceived by the midi-chlorians, as the prophecy foretold. Skywalker was [[Jedi training|trained as a Jedi]] by Jinn's [[Padawan]], [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]],<ref name="Episode I" /> but he fell to the dark side of the Force in his inability to let go of his lover, [[Padmé Amidala Naberrie|Padmé Amidala]], and in his self-hatred and lust for power, became [[Sith apprentice|apprenticed]] to the [[Dark Lord of the Sith]] [[Darth Sidious]] as Darth Vader.<ref name="Episode III" /> However, [[Standard year|years]] later,<ref name="Galactic Atlas">''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas]]''</ref> the son of Anakin Skywalker and Padmé Amidala, the [[Jedi Knight]] [[Luke Skywalker]], swayed Vader to sacrifice himself to destroy Darth Sidious.<ref name="Episode VI">{{Film|VI}}</ref> With the Sith extinct, Anakin Skywalker thus fulfilled the prophecy of the Chosen One and brought balance to the Force.<ref name="Luke Skywalker">{{Databank|luke-skywalker-biography-gallery|Luke Skywalker Biography Gallery}}</ref><ref name="Beware the Power of the Dark Side!">''[[Return of the Jedi: Beware the Power of the Dark Side!]]''</ref>

=====The dyad=====
{{Main|Force dyad}}
{{Quote|My mother was the daughter of Vader. Your father was the son of the Emperor. What Palpatine doesn't know is we're a dyad in the Force, Rey. Two that are one.|Kylo Ren, to Rey|<ref name="Episode IX">{{Film|IX}}</ref>}}
[[File:Ben-and-Rey-on-Exegol.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Rey, the granddaughter of Darth Sidious, and Ben Solo, the grandson of Anakin Skywalker, formed a rare and powerful Force dyad.]]
After the Chosen One restored balance to the Force, [[Force-sensitive]]s Ben Solo, the maternal grandson of Anakin Skywalker, and Rey, the secret paternal granddaughter of Darth Sidious,<ref name="Episode IX" /> who trained as a Jedi under Luke Skywalker<ref name="Episode VIII" /> and his sister, [[Leia Skywalker Organa Solo|Leia Organa]], came to the forefront of the [[First Order-Resistance War|war]] between the [[First Order]] and the [[Resistance]]. The two were eventually [[Force-bond|bonded]] by the Force into one entity before it; a unique [[Force dyad|dyadic]] Force-bond<ref name="TROS VD">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref> that transcended physical and mental barriers between the individuals. Through their bond, Ben Solo and Rey were able to destroy the last Sith Lord, a revived Darth Sidious, both in body and soul,<ref name="TSWB">''[[The Star Wars Book]]''</ref> and thus return balance to the Force as Anakin Skywalker had.<ref name="Episode IX" />

==Force powers==
{{Main|Force power}}
{{Quote|A Jedi uses the Force for knowledge and defense&hellip; never for attack.|Yoda, to Luke Skywalker|<ref name="Episode V" />}}
The light side of the Force was primarily used for defensive abilities as opposed to aggressive ones. According to Yoda, the dark side was not more powerful than the light side in and of itself, but was merely "quicker, easier, more seductive."<ref name="Episode V" /> Of course, by no means were the followers of light unable to wield the light as a tool for battle.<ref name="LotJ" /><ref name="Episode III" />

[[File:GarroLetsGo-2015StarWars30.jpg|thumb|left|280px|[[Garro]] restored balance to his world by letting go of his power, returning it to the rocks for life to live in harmony.]]
However, practitioners of the light side possessed abilities that users of the dark side often lacked. Light side practitioners were able to "heal" a [[Bleeding|bled]] kyber crystal, which were reddened by dark side users, restoring them to a pure form.<ref name="Ahsoka">[[Ahsoka (novel)|''Ahsoka'']]</ref> Some users of the light side knew the technique to [[Force healing|heal]] wounds.<ref name="Mandalorian Ch7">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 7: The Reckoning}}</ref> The Jedi Order also had an ancient technique called [[Morichro]], an art which allowed the user to slow down the bodily functions of an opponent to the point of [[death]]. [[Jedi Master|Master]] [[Yaddle]] was the only Jedi known to use this ability as it was very dangerous.<ref name="SWCE">''[[Star Wars Character Encyclopedia: Updated and Expanded]]''</ref> The light side could be used to form defensive barriers, such as the one used by [[Cere Junda]] to defend [[Cal Kestis]].<ref name="Star Wars Jedi: Fallen Order">''[[Star Wars Jedi: Fallen Order]]''</ref>

[[File:ThreeJediVisitors-RotJ40-Prouse.png|thumb|right|250px|Anakin Skywalker, Yoda, and Obi-Wan Kenobi retained their identities after death, becoming spirits in the Force.]]
An adherent of the light side could also master the ability of becoming a [[Force spirit]], which allowed the user to preserve their consciousness after death and manifest as a spirit to commune with the living, without the need of a relic binding them to they physical world. With this power, some Force spirits were capable of demonstrating extraordinary feats such as calling down [[Force lightning|lightning]] and interacting with the mortal world.<ref name="Episode VIII" /> Force spirits included [[Yoda]], [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]], [[Anakin Skywalker]],<ref name="Episode VI" /> [[Luke Skywalker]], and [[Leia Skywalker Organa Solo]].<ref name="Episode IX" />

[[Qui-Gon Jinn]] was the first person to discover this method of achieving [[immortality]], but died before mastering it. Jinn mastered eternal consciousness, but for a time he could not appear as a blue tinted version of his physical form and instead came back as a disembodied voice.<ref name="Voices">{{TCW|Voices}}</ref> Almost a decade later, Jinn mastered the intricacies of being a Force spirit, which he demonstrated when he appeared to Obi-Wan on Tatooine in a nearly corporeal form that was practically indistinguishable from how he had appeared in life.<ref name="Master and Apprentice">{{StoryCite|story=Master and Apprentice (short story)|book=From a Certain Point of View}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
In the [[Adventures of the Starkiller, Episode I: The Star Wars|second draft]] of the script for [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''A New Hope'']], the light side of the Force is called "the Ashla". "The Ashla" was [[canon]]ized as a name for the light side in ''[[Star Wars Rebels: Steps Into Shadow]]''.

===Origin===
====George Lucas====
[[George Lucas]], the writer of the [[Original trilogy|original]] and [[Prequel trilogy|prequel]] trilogies, defined the light side of the Force as selflessness, joy, spiritual, everlasting, and difficult to achieve&mdash;good, as opposed to the dark side, which individuals fell to through possessiveness, greed, and in their inability to let go, and was defined by Lucas as selfish, pleasure, biological, temporary, and easy to achieve; evil. In addition, George Lucas stated that the dark side could only be overcome through discipline.<ref name="GL Force">''[[Star Wars: The Complete Saga]]'' (Star Wars Episode V: George Lucas On The Force)</ref><ref name="Making of ROTS">''[[The Making of Star Wars Revenge of the Sith]]''</ref> Joy was defined by George Lucas as giving oneself to others&mdash;compassion, which is everlasting<ref name="Happiness speech">{{YouTube|video=sCP2SGTIz28|text=George Lucas' Advice|channel=channel/UCGiByAxbLt1wzYlC2Dv6ZEw|name=Colorado Flier}}</ref> and is a component of the mental state of happiness besides the emotion of pleasure,<ref name="Happiness interview">{{YouTube|video=2TdGd0MlmvI|text=George Lucas: Project Happiness Interview|channel=channel/UCOLrMxi9peCxmLA2qBUKK5w|name=Mount Madonna School Values in World Thought}}</ref> which Lucas believed was short-lived, purely self-centered, the peak of which could only be reached once, and was created by a self-centered motive of greed.<ref name="Happiness speech" />

When an individual attempts to sustain the peak pleasure, Lucas explained his belief, the individual is doomed; one must let it go.<ref name="Happiness speech" /> Thus, as he found that an individual had a compassionate side and a self-centered passionate side, a dichotomy between selflessness and selfishness, George Lucas believed that the two sides must be kept in balance for one to do good things; that one's innate greed must be controlled to prevent one from becoming evil and suffer as a result of hatred, as a result of anger, fear, which stems from the inability to let go of things being taken away.<ref name="Bill Bradley">{{WebCite|url=https://soundcloud.com/billbradley/american-voices-george-lucas-special|text=American Voices - George Lucas Special by Senator Bill Bradley &#124; Free Listening on SoundCloud|author=Bradley, Bill|date=2015-11-15|archivedate=20170315141932}}</ref>

====Duality in mythology====
By [[Wikipedia:Joseph Campbell|Joseph Campbell's]] observations, the dualistic nature of the light and dark sides of the Force follows the [[Wikipedia:Hinduism|Hindu]]<ref name="Power of Myth 2">{{YouTube|video=bqE1dwDvNZw|text=The Power of Myth: The Message of the Myth (Episode 2)|channel=channel/UCP_N_C3LpjctLc5ndWT8CFA|name=Zen Lokamaya}}</ref> [[Wikipedia:Trimurti|trimurti]] [[Wikipedia:Sadasiva|Sadashiva]]<ref name="Elephanta Island, Britannica">{{WebCite|url=https://www.britannica.com/place/Elephanta-Island|text=Elephanta Island|archivedate=20200917171650}}</ref> sculpture of the [[Wikipedia:Elephanta Caves|Elephanta Caves]], where one finds themself in a field of dual nature and time when one moves out of the eternal transcendence. This dichotomous nature of reality is one perceived by humanity, Campbell states, and duality is only a human apparition, limited to the field of time. Joseph Campbell saw that man's mythology is based on the inside of duality, leading to religions having a tendency to be ethical&mdash;sin and atonement, right and wrong. The Bible draws a distinction between God and nature, pitting nature against man and differentiating man from God. The condemnation of nature, Campbell observed, originates from [[Wikipedia:Zoroaster|Zarathustra's]] time and is shared by Islam, yet it is not shared by [[Wikipedia:Shinto|Shinto]], basic Hinduism, or Buddhism. In the latter beliefs, God is nature, and the force's "sides" are only artificial, temporal constructs of humanity.<ref name="Power of Myth 2" /> Philosopher [[Wikipedia:Friedrich Nietzsche|Friedrich Nietzsche]] records his personal characterization of Zarathustra in his novel [[Wikipedia:Thus Spoke Zarathustra|''Thus Spake Zarathustra'']], where he posits the [[Wikipedia:Übermensch|Übermensch]] as a goal for humanity, the [[Wikipedia:Eternal return|eternal recurrence]] through space and time<ref name="Zarathustra">[[Wikipedia:Thus Spoke Zarathustra|''Thus Spake Zarathustra'']]</ref>&mdash;which is echoed by the course of events in ''Star Wars''<ref name="Episode I" /><ref name="Episode IV" /><ref name="Episode VI" />&mdash;and criticizes the dichotomous morality of good and evil.<ref name="Zarathustra" />

===Conveyance===
In ''Star Wars''{{'s}} [[musical score]]s, the light side of the Force is signified through [[The Force Theme|the Force motif]]. As music theorist James Buhler explains in ''Music and Cinema'', where the Force [[Wikipedia:Leitmotif|leitmotif]] first sounds in the Binary Sunset sequence of [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''Star Wars'': Episode IV ''A New Hope'']], there is no apparent link between Luke Skywalker looking out to the horizon of his [[desert]] wasteland home and the Force, which is introduced subsequent to the scene. Such moments of inconsistency between the signifier and the signified gives the ''Star Wars'' films their mythical character, Buhler explains, as the failure of music in its semiotic function makes it transcendent of immediate human comprehension. ''Star Wars''{{'s}} transcendental light side music is contrasted by dark side themes such as "[[The Imperial March]]," which strictly adheres to the signified&mdash;here, the leitmotif no longer signifies what lies beyond signification. No longer communicting mythically, the music intensifies in the opposition between the light side and the dark.<ref name="SWMM">Buhler, James. 2000. "[https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=bo8PcOeUdX4C&printsec=frontcover&dq=Star+Wars,+Music+and+Myth&hl=en&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=Star%20Wars%2C%20Music%20and%20Myth&f=false Star Wars, Music and Myth]." In Music and Cinema, Buhler, Flinn, Neumeyer eds.. Wesleyan University Press</ref>

James Buhler further analyses that the light side is signified in music through a naturalized order, whilst the dark's is emphasised in music through an imposed order. The mechanical and unnatural leitmotif of "The Imperial March" defines the dark side as evil, which highlights that Luke Skywalker stopped seeing the Force as a technological tool to dominate Darth Vader's power and defeat the Sith only after suffering the loss of his hand<ref name="SWMM" /> and letting go of his [[Skywalker lightsaber|lightsaber]].<ref name="SW 2020 4">[[Star Wars (2020) 4|''Star Wars'' (2020) 4]]</ref>

